"""Original network-practice and reflective-practice course drafts."""

def courses(c,m):
    return [
c("SWP-040","Referral Networks and Interprofessional Practice","referral-networks-and-interprofessional-practice","Referral Networks\nand Interprofessional\nPractice",
  "Build a reviewable cross-sector referral arrangement with clear roles, accessible pathways and accountable follow-up.",[
m("Service ecosystems and referral readiness","Check a service pathway against purpose, eligibility, capacity and the person's preferences.",[
"A referral network connects organisations and roles that contribute different forms of support. It may include public services, community organisations, disability advocates, education providers and livelihood programmes. Begin with the person's chosen goal rather than a list of institutions. Ask which contribution is needed, whether the person wants that support and whether another route is more suitable. A directory is a starting point for checking an option; the presence of a name in it does not prove that a service is available or appropriate. [S1, S2]",
"A useful directory records the service purpose, eligibility, geographic reach, access arrangements, fees if any, referral route, contact role and date of verification. Check capacity and waiting arrangements through an authorised contact before describing an option as available. A service may have changed its criteria or temporarily stopped receiving referrals. Keep sensitive information about individuals out of the directory. Assign responsibility for checking entries and recording corrections, so that an obsolete contact does not become a repeated barrier for everyone using the network. [S2]",
"Examine the pathway from the person's perspective. A technically eligible service can remain inaccessible because of transport, communication, opening hours, documentation requirements or cost. Ask which barrier matters in this situation and whether a reasonable adjustment or alternative route can be agreed. Do not promise admission on another organisation's behalf. The navigation umbrella review describes varied roles and arrangements across studies; it does not establish one model that will work in every setting. Use it to ask practical questions about local access and responsibility. [R3]",
"Network work differs from taking over every task in a person's case. A worker can improve the shared route while an identified practitioner remains responsible for the individual support plan. Discuss the proposed route with the person, explain what remains uncertain and agree the next contact. If a directory entry fails a check, record the specific issue and notify the responsible role through the approved process. Immediate safeguarding concerns require the relevant response procedure rather than waiting for a routine network update. [S1, S2]"],
"Fictional case: a learner finds a livelihood service in an old directory. The current contact confirms a waiting list and a changed document requirement. The worker checks the person's preferred option and discusses an accessible alternative rather than promising immediate admission.",
"Draft a directory entry for a fictional service. Include eligibility, access, capacity status, verification date and update owner. Identify one question to discuss with the person before referral.",
"A strong entry distinguishes confirmed information from an unresolved condition. It records who will update the route and avoids promising another service's decision.",["S1","S2","R3"],[
("The person's goal guides the service search.","Several organisations offer different support. What should guide the first referral discussion?","The person's goal, preferences and the contribution actually needed.",["The organisation with the most familiar name.","The first contact listed regardless of service purpose.","A requirement to use every available organisation."],"Service matching begins with the purpose and the person's participation."),
("Directory entries require current verification.","An old directory lists a service as open. What should the worker do before promising access?","Check its current eligibility, capacity and referral arrangements.",["Assume the printed entry remains accurate.","Promise a place because the organisation previously accepted referrals.","Remove the person's choice to make the referral faster."],"A directory identifies an option, not a guaranteed place."),
("Eligibility and practical accessibility are different questions.","A person meets a service's criteria but cannot use its transport route. What needs attention?","The access barrier and possible adjustments or alternatives.",["Eligibility alone as proof that access is solved.","The assumption that the person has rejected support.","An admission promise without discussing travel."],"Meeting formal criteria does not remove practical barriers."),
("A network needs an owner for directory updates.","The same incorrect contact repeatedly blocks referrals. Which change addresses the shared problem?","Assign an update owner and a dated verification process.",["Ask each person to discover the error independently.","Keep the entry unchanged because it was once correct.","Store private case histories in the public directory."],"Shared maintenance prevents an obsolete entry being repeatedly relied on."),
("Navigation evidence does not establish one universally effective model.","What can the navigation umbrella review support when designing a local pathway?","Questions about roles, context and access that need local checking.",["A guarantee that one arrangement works everywhere.","Proof that a listed service has current capacity.","Permission to skip the person's preferences."],"The review describes varied evidence and arrangements, requiring contextual judgement."),
]),
m("Roles handovers and shared accountability","Agree a bounded interprofessional handover with a named receiving role and clear follow-up.",[
"Interprofessional practice requires more than copying several organisations into a message. Participants need to understand the purpose of collaboration, their own contribution and the limits of their authority. Identify which role coordinates the next step, which service makes an eligibility decision and who will communicate with the person. Avoid transferring responsibility through an unexplained phrase such as 'referred on'. A referral can be sent without being received, understood or accepted. Record these stages separately and agree what will happen if the receiving role cannot respond. [S2]",
"Before a handover, check the applicable information-sharing authority and the person's understanding through the approved process. Share the relevant information needed for the justified purpose using an authorised route. A handover summary can state the person's goal, relevant context, agreed request, immediate concerns and the contact responsible for clarification. Do not include an entire file simply because it is available. Where different services use different terms, explain the meaning rather than assume that a shared word represents the same assessment or decision. [S1, S2]",
"Professional mandates can overlap or conflict. A worker may face an institutional deadline while a person needs time to consider options. Discuss the actual tension, the relevant duties and the decision that each role can make. The German rehabilitation study examines such mandate tensions in a particular health setting. It offers a lens for reflection, not a tested solution for every Nigerian cross-sector partnership. Ask what requires consultation, which decision must be escalated and how the person's account will remain visible in a joint discussion. [R2]",
"A useful handover closes the communication loop. Confirm receipt and the next responsible role, explain the agreed next step to the person and set a review date. If no response arrives, follow the escalation route rather than treating silence as acceptance. Keep a proportionate record of the request, response and outstanding work. This course addresses service relationships across sectors; the healthcare collaboration course examines team practice within health settings, while generalist case management addresses the person's overall support plan. These activities can support one another without becoming interchangeable. [S2, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a housing worker sends an agreed referral to a training provider. The provider confirms receipt but needs a separate eligibility decision. The two roles identify who will contact the person, when a decision is expected and how an unanswered request will be escalated.",
"Write a short handover agreement. State the request, relevant information, receiving role, decision owner, response date and fallback if the referral is not accepted.",
"Receipt and acceptance should be distinct. A good agreement makes responsibilities and the person's next contact clear, with an authorised information-sharing route.",["S2","S1","R2"],[
("Sending a referral does not prove acceptance.","A message has been sent but no response received. Which status is accurate?","Sent, with receipt and acceptance still unconfirmed.",["Accepted because the sender completed the form.","Completed because another service was named.","Rejected because no immediate answer arrived."],"Each stage needs its own evidence and follow-up."),
("Handover information follows a justified purpose and authorised route.","What should determine the information in an interagency handover?","The relevant request, applicable authority and minimum information needed.",["Every detail held by the sending service.","A recipient's general interest in the file.","Whatever can be sent fastest through a personal account."],"Collaboration does not remove confidentiality or proportionate information handling."),
("Roles and decision authority should be explicit.","Two services each expect the other to contact the person. What should be agreed?","A named contact role, next action and review date.",["A general statement that the network will manage it.","A duplicate referral with no responsibility discussion.","Silence until the person asks again."],"Explicit ownership reduces gaps between organisations."),
("Mandate tensions require contextual consultation.","An institutional deadline conflicts with a person's need to consider options. What is appropriate?","Identify the duties and decision authority and seek timely consultation.",["Assume the institutional deadline removes the person's voice.","Treat one overseas study as a universal resolution.","Ignore every deadline without checking its basis."],"The tension requires a reasoned local decision and a visible account of participation."),
("An unanswered handover requires an agreed fallback.","A receiving role misses the agreed response date. What should the sending worker use?","The documented follow-up and escalation arrangement.",["An assumption that silence means consent.","A completed-case label regardless of the person's position.","Unrestricted distribution of the file to find any response."],"A closed communication loop includes a way to handle non-response."),
]),
m("Network review and access improvement","Use proportionate pathway data and participant feedback to identify a specific improvement without overstating causation.",[
"A network should review whether its arrangements help people reach the intended support. Start with a clear question, such as whether referrals are acknowledged or whether a document requirement prevents access. Choose a small set of measures that match that question. Counts of referrals sent, acknowledgements received, decisions made and services reached describe different stages. They should not be combined into a single success total without explaining the definitions. A large number sent may coexist with long waits or low service uptake. [S2, R3]",
"Ask for the person's experience as well as administrative counts. They may describe unclear instructions, repeated storytelling, inaccessible communication or an option that did not fit their priorities. Gather this information through an approved and proportionate process, with attention to confidentiality and the burden of participation. Avoid displaying identifiable case details in a shared network report. Differences between groups may indicate a barrier worth investigating, but small numbers, missing responses and changing eligibility can affect what a comparison means. Record those limitations alongside the finding. [S1, S2]",
"Select an improvement that responds to a verified problem. If a referral form repeatedly omits a necessary access preference, the network could clarify the form and the receiving role's response process. Agree who will change the arrangement, what evidence will show whether the change is useful and when it will be reviewed. The IJSDS institutional-corruption paper offers a contextual discussion of accountability and institutional barriers, not a causal evaluation of a referral reform. Do not convert a conceptual framework or a before-and-after count into proof of effectiveness. [R5, R3]",
"Keep an honest account of outcomes and unresolved work. A change in uptake may reflect the network adjustment, seasonal demand, staffing, transport or another influence. Ask what alternative explanations remain and whether the person reports a meaningful improvement. Retain a route for complaints and corrections, including feedback that challenges the network's preferred account. Document what will be continued, revised or stopped through the authorised process. Network learning should improve shared access while keeping responsibility for individual support and urgent concerns with the appropriate practitioner or service. [S1, S2]"],
"Fictional case: a network sends more referrals after revising its form, but service uptake is unchanged. Feedback suggests that appointment hours still conflict with caring responsibilities. The review separates referral volume from access and agrees a focused discussion with the receiving service.",
"Propose three pathway measures, one feedback question and a review date. Explain one alternative reason why an improvement in uptake would not by itself prove that the new form caused the change.",
"Strong reviews distinguish stages, include the person's experience and acknowledge competing explanations. An improvement plan has an owner and a measurable review question.",["S2","S1","R3","R5"],[
("Referral volume and service access are different measures.","A network sends more referrals but uptake is unchanged. Which conclusion is justified?","Referral activity increased while access needs further investigation.",["Every extra referral represents successful support.","The new form necessarily caused improved wellbeing.","Administrative volume makes participant feedback unnecessary."],"The stages answer different questions and should not be conflated."),
("Participant feedback complements pathway counts.","Counts show timely acknowledgements but people report confusing instructions. What should the review do?","Examine the reported experience alongside the administrative measures.",["Discard the feedback because acknowledgement was timely.","Assume all people experienced the same problem.","Publish identifiable complaints to demonstrate openness."],"Counts do not capture every relevant aspect of access or experience."),
("Small samples and missing responses limit comparisons.","Only a few people answer a pathway survey. How should its findings be reported?","With the response coverage and limits visible.",["As a complete account of every network user.","Without mentioning non-response.","As proof of a population-wide causal effect."],"Evidence coverage affects the strength of the conclusion."),
("An improvement plan needs ownership and a review question.","A recurring form problem has been verified. Which next step is most useful?","Agree an owner, a specific change and a way to review its usefulness.",["Issue a general request for everyone to collaborate better.","Declare success before implementing a change.","Remove every measurement to avoid disagreement."],"An actionable change can be followed through and assessed."),
("Before-and-after observations do not establish causation alone.","Uptake improves after a network change. What should the report consider?","Other changes and limitations before attributing the improvement.",["The timing as complete proof of causation.","Only evidence that supports the preferred explanation.","The conceptual paper as a trial of this local change."],"Alternative explanations remain relevant when no causal design isolates the effect."),
]),
]),
c("SWP-041","Reflective Practice Boundaries and Supervision","reflective-practice-boundaries-and-supervision","Reflective Practice\nBoundaries and Supervision",
  "Use structured reflection and supervision to examine judgement, maintain professional boundaries and agree a reviewable learning action.",[
m("Structured reflection and accountable judgement","Separate description, interpretation and learning when reviewing a practice decision.",[
"Reflection examines how a decision was made and what could improve future practice. It begins with a specific situation rather than a general judgement that a worker is good or bad. Describe the relevant event, the person's account, the action taken and what was known at the time. Then distinguish observation from interpretation and hindsight. A later outcome may reveal a learning need without proving that every earlier uncertainty could have been resolved. Use a fictional or appropriately de-identified account in learning settings. [S1, S3]",
"Ask which assumptions shaped attention and whose perspective was missing. A worker may interpret a missed appointment as unwillingness while overlooking cost, fear, an inaccessible message or an error in the service's timetable. Reflection should open these possibilities to checking rather than replace one unsupported explanation with another. Consider how power, identity and organisational expectations influenced the interaction. Seek the person's view where appropriate through an approved process. A reflective account remains accountable for actions while recognising that institutional conditions can constrain the options available. [S1, S3]",
"A structured note can move through description, interpretation, alternative explanations, relevant duties and a next learning action. Include evidence that challenges the preferred explanation. Identify a question for supervision rather than attempting to solve every dilemma alone. The moral-distress review describes professional and organisational tensions across settings outside Nigeria. It helps frame questions about ethical strain; its qualitative synthesis does not diagnose a particular worker or prove that one reflective exercise will prevent distress. Keep individual learning and organisational responsibility visible together. [R1]",
"Finish with a practical action that can be revisited. This may involve checking a policy, practising an explanation, seeking feedback or escalating an access problem to the appropriate role. State who will act and when the learning will be reviewed. Reflection is not a substitute for reporting an urgent concern, correcting an inaccurate record or following an incident procedure. If the review reveals a current risk or duty requiring action, use the relevant response route promptly. An eloquent reflective note cannot replace the necessary practical response. [S1, S3]"],
"Fictional case: a worker initially describes missed appointments as disengagement. A review finds that the service sent instructions in an inaccessible format. The worker separates the initial assumption from the evidence and agrees to check communication preferences before the next contact.",
"Write a five-part reflective note: event, interpretation, alternative explanation, duty or uncertainty, and a specific learning action with a review date.",
"A strong note challenges an assumption without inventing a new fact. It links reflection to action and identifies any issue that requires immediate reporting or consultation.",["S1","S3","R1"],[
("Reflection separates description from interpretation.","A worker reviews a missed appointment. Which entry is descriptive?","The appointment was missed and the reason has not yet been established.",["The person is uninterested in all support.","The missed visit proves that the service is ineffective.","The worker must have known every barrier in advance."],"A descriptive starting point keeps unverified explanations separate."),
("Alternative explanations need checking rather than automatic acceptance.","Reflection suggests that transport may explain an absence. What should happen next?","Check the possibility with the person and relevant information.",["Replace the original assumption with transport as a confirmed fact.","Avoid asking because reflection already established the cause.","Remove the person's account from the review."],"A plausible explanation remains a hypothesis until appropriately examined."),
("Power and organisational context can shape judgement.","A review focuses only on a worker's attitude while a service barrier remains. What should be added?","An examination of the barrier and the roles able to address it.",["A claim that organisational context removes all individual responsibility.","A conclusion that only personal resilience matters.","A diagnosis of every worker from the same event."],"Individual accountability and organisational conditions can both matter."),
("A learning action should be specific and reviewable.","Which reflective conclusion provides a clear next step?","Check communication preferences at the next contact and review this with the supervisor.",["Try to be better at everything.","Avoid all future uncertainty.","Assume the written reflection completes every duty."],"A defined action makes follow-through possible."),
("Reflection does not replace an urgent response duty.","A reflective discussion reveals an immediate concern requiring the service's response procedure. What is appropriate?","Use that procedure promptly while arranging subsequent reflection.",["Wait for the next group meeting before taking any action.","Treat a reflective paragraph as an incident response.","Keep the concern within a learning journal only."],"Urgent responsibilities require the relevant action route."),
]),
m("Supervision agreements and professional boundaries","Prepare a supervision question and recognise when boundary concerns require authorised advice or escalation.",[
"Supervision provides a structured opportunity to review practice, responsibilities and development with an authorised supervisor. Clarify its purpose, frequency, record arrangements, confidentiality limits and escalation routes. Different settings may combine supportive, educational and managerial functions in different ways. A peer conversation can be helpful without carrying the authority of formal supervision. Confirm who can make a decision, approve an action or address an employment concern. Do not assume that a discussion group fulfils every supervision requirement simply because participants find it supportive. [S3, R7]",
"Prepare a focused agenda rather than an unfiltered case history. State the decision or uncertainty, relevant facts, the person's priorities, steps already taken and the advice sought. Share sensitive information only through the authorised arrangement. Ask the supervisor to distinguish guidance from a decision that they are responsible for making. Record the agreed action, owner and review point through the approved process. Where advice conflicts with a duty or a serious concern remains unresolved, use the relevant escalation route and retain a proportionate account of the issue. [S1, S3]",
"Professional boundaries help maintain a relationship directed toward the person's legitimate service needs. A gift, private contact request, financial request or overlapping community relationship may require careful discussion rather than an impulsive promise or a universal rule invented by the learner. Identify the power imbalance, potential conflict, applicable policy and effect on the person. Seek timely supervision before acting when possible. Avoid using the relationship for personal benefit, exposing confidential information or making contact through an unapproved private channel merely because it feels friendly. [S1]",
"Supervision must remain connected to the actual concern. If a person reports harm, the response may require safeguarding, complaints or another authorised process alongside learning support. A group cannot promise absolute secrecy where relevant duties apply. The reflective-group review maps diverse interventions and mostly qualitative evidence; it does not prove that one group format supplies formal oversight or solves retention problems. Use that evidence to ask about facilitation, psychological safety, accountability and evaluation, while checking the organisation's supervision arrangements and the person's protection needs. [R7, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a person asks a worker to become a private paid adviser outside the service. The worker does not make an immediate promise. They explain that the request needs an authorised boundary and conflict review and bring a focused question to supervision.",
"Prepare a supervision agenda for the case. Include the decision needed, relevant policy question, potential conflict, information-sharing limits and an action route if the concern remains unresolved.",
"A useful agenda identifies the authority needed and the risk of personal benefit or conflicting roles. A supportive discussion alone does not establish approval for an outside arrangement.",["S3","R7","S1"],[
("Supportive peer discussion and authorised supervision have different functions.","A peer group provides emotional support. What must still be checked?","Whether formal oversight and required decision authority are provided elsewhere.",["Whether feeling supported automatically fulfils every supervision requirement.","Whether peers can approve any action without an assigned role.","Whether group membership removes professional accountability."],"Support does not by itself confer managerial or professional authority."),
("A supervision agenda identifies a specific decision or uncertainty.","Which preparation best supports a focused supervision session?","Relevant facts, the person's priorities and the advice or decision sought.",["An unrestricted file copy with no review question.","Only a request for reassurance without identifying the concern.","A final decision disguised as a request that cannot be challenged."],"A clear question helps the supervisor address the actual need."),
("Boundary decisions require policy and conflict review.","A service user proposes a private paid arrangement with the worker. What should the worker do?","Seek authorised advice about boundaries and conflicts before agreeing.",["Accept because the person made the request voluntarily.","Assume payment is acceptable whenever it is small.","Use a private channel to avoid the service's review process."],"A request can still create a conflict or inappropriate personal benefit."),
("Confidentiality in supervision has relevant limits.","A learning group is asked to promise absolute secrecy about every disclosure. What is appropriate?","Explain the agreed confidentiality arrangements and relevant reporting limits.",["Promise secrecy even where duties require action.","Share all case details outside the group without authority.","Assume calling it reflection removes every duty."],"The learning setting does not erase applicable protection and reporting responsibilities."),
("Unresolved serious concerns require an escalation route.","A supervision discussion leaves a serious duty-related concern unresolved. What should the worker use?","The relevant authorised escalation process with a proportionate record.",["Silence because supervision occurred once.","A personal public post containing case details.","An assumption that attendance transfers all responsibility."],"Supervision supports judgement but does not remove the need for appropriate escalation."),
]),
m("Feedback learning and organisational follow-through","Turn feedback into a bounded improvement and distinguish supportive claims from evidence of effectiveness.",[
"Feedback can come from the person receiving support, a supervisor, a colleague or a structured review. Ask about a specific behaviour or decision so that the response can guide action. Invite correction without pressuring someone to praise the service or disclose more than they wish. Clarify how feedback will be used and what route exists for a complaint or a concern requiring a formal response. A learner should not treat a person's participation in feedback as agreement with every decision made about their support. [S1, S3]",
"Respond by checking the meaning of the feedback and separating it from a global judgement about competence. Ask what happened, what effect was reported and which part can be changed. If accounts differ, retain the distinction and seek appropriate clarification rather than choosing the most flattering version. Agree a practice change within the worker's authority and identify issues needing organisational action. The professional-mandate study highlights tensions between client, professional and institutional expectations in German rehabilitation. It supports contextual questioning rather than a universal explanation of every disagreement. [R2]",
"A learning plan should state the skill or issue, the practice activity, the source of support and the evidence for review. For example, a worker might rehearse an accessible explanation, use it in an appropriate setting and seek feedback on whether the next step was understood. Do not claim that confidence alone establishes competence or that one positive comment proves improved outcomes. Where a planned change affects people or records, use the authorised process and supervision. Keep the review proportionate and avoid collecting unnecessary personal information for a learning exercise. [S1, S3]",
"Organisational follow-through matters when recurring barriers exceed one worker's authority. Record the issue through the relevant route, identify the role able to respond and agree when the concern will be reviewed. A supportive reflective group may provide learning opportunities, but the scoping review found limited evidence on retention and cost-effectiveness. Do not sell reflection as a proven remedy for staffing shortages or unsafe conditions. Review whether the intended practice action occurred, what changed and what remains uncertain, while preserving space for feedback that challenges the preferred conclusion. [R7, R1]"],
"Fictional case: feedback says that a worker explained options clearly but the appointment letter remained inaccessible. The learning plan includes a communication rehearsal and a request to the authorised role to review the letter format. Confidence and service access are reviewed separately.",
"Write a learning plan with one individual skill action and one organisational issue. Give an owner, review evidence, date and a limit on what a positive result would establish.",
"A strong plan distinguishes the worker's authority from an organisational change and uses specific review evidence. It does not infer overall competence or causal impact from confidence alone.",["S1","S3","R2","R7","R1"],[
("Specific feedback is more actionable than a global label.","Which feedback request best supports learning about an explanation?","Which part of the next step was clear and which part needed clarification?",["Would you describe the worker as perfect?","Can you confirm that every service decision was correct?","Will you avoid mentioning problems in the review?"],"A focused question invites useful detail without demanding praise."),
("Feedback participation does not equal agreement with every decision.","A person answers a feedback question but disputes the support plan. What should the worker recognise?","Their feedback and disagreement can both be recorded and addressed.",["Answering the survey means the plan is accepted.","Disagreement makes all feedback invalid.","Only positive comments belong in the record."],"Participation does not surrender the person's ability to challenge a decision."),
("Confidence alone is not evidence of competence or outcomes.","A worker feels more confident after a reflective session. What can be concluded?","Confidence changed; practice and outcome claims need appropriate evidence.",["Every practice skill is now independently verified.","Service-user outcomes necessarily improved.","No further supervision can be needed."],"A self-report of confidence answers a narrower question than competence or effect."),
("Learning plans distinguish individual and organisational authority.","An inaccessible standard letter exceeds a learner's authority to redesign. What should the plan include?","A relevant skill action and a referral of the letter issue to the authorised role.",["A promise to change every organisational process alone.","Only a personal attitude goal while ignoring the recurring barrier.","An unapproved change to every live record."],"A bounded plan links individual learning to the role able to address a systemic issue."),
("Reflective-group evidence does not prove a remedy for staffing problems.","A service proposes reflection as a guaranteed retention solution. What is the evidence-aware response?","Review its learning purpose and the limited retention evidence while addressing organisational conditions.",["Guarantee retention because groups may feel supportive.","Replace every formal supervision arrangement with a group.","Treat a scoping review as a local causal trial."],"The mapped evidence does not justify a universal retention guarantee."),
]),
]),
    ]
